<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">London Convention (1884)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Convention of London (1884)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Convention was an agreement signed on 27 February 1884 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> delegation led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria-convention">Pretoria Convention</a> of 1881, restored the name South African Republic, redrew its western border and dropped any mention of British suzerainty, leaving Britain only a veto over the republic's treaties with foreign states other than the Orange Free State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london-convention-1884.jpg" alt="London Convention (1884)" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Boer_Delegates,_1883-1884.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
